      "history_summaries": [
        "Repair round 1/3: Replace all 5 assertNextStepWhen calls and the astRelaxedLiveness call in controlvis.scala with manually implemented assertions. Error category: assertion_error — Chisel-FV's delayedBool + when-guard pattern is improperly lowered, losing the antecedent register and guard. Fix uses RegNext to create a 1-cycle-delayed antecedent register, then fvAssert with a combinatorial implication (!ant || consequent). This produces correct Verilog with a proper register and a simple combinatorial assertion. For return_to_idle_bounded, a counter tracks cycles since last IDLE (fails if >50). All labels preserved exactly.",
        "Repair round 2/3: Fix two remaining assertion failures (read_miss_to_read_data_on_done, return_to_idle_bounded) by adding io.Rst_n guards. Error type: assertion_error for both. For read_miss_to_read_data_on_done (line 157): added `|| !io.Rst_n` to the assertion consequent — when reset is active, stateReg legitimately goes to IDLE instead of READ_DATA because the DUT's `when(!io.Rst_n)` block has highest priority. For return_to_idle_bounded (line 194): added `!io.Rst_n ||` guard — when reset is active, the FSM may have been out of IDLE for many cycles before reset takes effect, and the bounded counter check should not apply during reset cycles. Both fixes are assertion_error corrections: the DUT correctly implements reset priority, and the assertions need to account for this legitimate reset behavior. Target assertion labels preserved exactly: 'read_miss_to_read_data_on_done' and 'return_to_idle_bounded'. Homologous assertions: for read_miss_to_read_data_on_done — no structurally identical assertions in source (other assertions have different consequent states or unconditional transitions); for return_to_idle_bounded — no structurally identical assertions (unique bounded-liveness counter).",
        "Repair round 3/3: Fix `read_miss_to_read_data_on_done` assertion by registering the reset guard. Error type: assertion_error — the antecedent `ant` samples DUT conditions at cycle N via RegNext, checking the consequent at cycle N+1. The old guard `!io.Rst_n` checked the reset at cycle N+1 (consequent evaluation time), but the reset that caused the DUT to go to IDLE instead of READ_DATA was asserted at cycle N. The fix adds `val wasReset = RegNext(!io.Rst_n, false.B)` so the guard captures `io.Rst_n` from cycle N, the same cycle the antecedent sampled. At the failing time 30ns: wasReset = RegNext(!io.Rst_n at time 20) = !0 = 1, so `!ant || (stateReg===READ_DATA) || wasReset` = `0 || false || 1` = true. Target assertion label 'read_miss_to_read_data_on_done' preserved exactly. No homologous assertions: only this assertion expects READ_DATA as consequent; all others (read_hit_to_idle, read_data_to_idle, write_hit_to_idle_on_done, write_miss_to_idle_on_done) expect IDLE, which matches the reset behavior, so they do not need the same registered-reset guard."
      ],
